1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Shaping Up.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,440
It's one of the most enduring human obsessions.

3
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:15,980
We'll do almost anything to get from here to here.

4
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:21,360
Fitness gurus like Jack LaLanne and Jane Fonda

5
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:25,420
made it a mission and an industry.

6
00:00:25,421 --> 00:00:30,420
Now, the quest for health on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,520
Physical fitness.

8
00:00:52,521 --> 00:00:54,980
It comes in as many shapes and sizes

9
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:58,360
as the people who pursue it.

10
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:03,560
It's aggressive aerobic boxing and the fine muscle control

11
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:04,860
of meditative yoga.

12
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:12,640
It's a battery of machines capable of building bodies

13
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:16,080
with assembly line efficiency.

14
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:20,440
It's luxurious health clubs where sweating's a social phenomenon

15
00:01:20,441 --> 00:01:22,800
and computers click off the calories.

16
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:28,880
It can be the pursuit of lifelong health

17
00:01:28,881 --> 00:01:31,100
or just plain strange,

18
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:33,580
but its benefits cannot be denied.

19
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:35,200
Keeps you alive.

20
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:36,140
You handle yourself better.

21
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:37,000
You can eat more food.

22
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:38,060
Everything about you is better.

23
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:40,880
See, you don't get old from age.

24
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,240
You get old from inactivity.

25
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:45,340
See, the only way you can hurt this body, don't do it.

26
00:01:45,341 --> 00:01:47,080
Sit around on your big fat behind

27
00:01:47,081 --> 00:01:48,620
and think of the good old days

28
00:01:48,621 --> 00:01:52,860
or make some excuse why you're not doing it.

29
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:56,020
While exercise technology might change,

30
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:59,860
the desire or even obsession to stay fit is hardly new.

31
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:05,900
Much of what drives physical fitness today

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,140
originated in the earliest cradles of civilization.

33
00:02:09,141 --> 00:02:19,380
Ancient Asia gave birth to some of the most effective methods

34
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:23,180
for muscle toning, reducing weight, and increasing

35
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,820
flexibility.

36
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:29,260
Yoga originated in India and has been dated back

37
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,760
to as far as 500 BC.

38
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:35,600
Though its purpose was less a form of exercise

39
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:38,240
than it was a meditative physical representation

40
00:02:38,241 --> 00:02:39,740
of Hindu philosophy.

41
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:46,180
Similarly, a variety of unarmed fighting methods influenced

42
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:51,180
by Zen Buddhism were also developed in India.

43
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:54,820
By 500 AD, the martial arts had spread to China,

44
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:58,120
where Buddhist monks practiced the graceful motions both

45
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:02,360
for exercise and as a lethal form of self-defense

46
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:04,960
against bandits.

47
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:07,240
But far older is the athletic training

48
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:08,980
of the Western world.

49
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:16,180
It dates back to at least 776 BC and the early Olympic Games.

50
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,120
Greek athletes achieved remarkable conditioning

51
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:22,520
through rigorous exercise that included rope climbing

52
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:25,700
and one of the earliest forms of weight training.

53
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:28,160
Sculpted from heavy stone or wood,

54
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:32,260
weights called haltieres were used much like modern dumbbells.

55
00:03:32,261 --> 00:03:33,160
Bells.

56
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:38,400
Beyond recognizing its value in sports,

57
00:03:38,401 --> 00:03:41,040
the Greeks, specifically the Athenians,

58
00:03:41,041 --> 00:03:44,840
also drew the earliest connection between exercise

59
00:03:44,841 --> 00:03:45,980
and health.

60
00:03:45,981 --> 00:03:49,240
By 400 BC, the famous physician Hippocrates

61
00:03:49,241 --> 00:03:52,980
was writing extensively on the value of physical fitness

62
00:03:52,981 --> 00:03:54,920
for everyone.

63
00:03:54,921 --> 00:03:58,480
Hippocrates was saying that exercise

64
00:03:58,481 --> 00:04:02,860
was crucial in maintaining health and preventing

65
00:04:02,861 --> 00:04:05,020
the onset of disease.

66
00:04:05,021 --> 00:04:07,660
He also wrote about taking long walks

67
00:04:07,661 --> 00:04:10,980
and running to increase perspiration,

68
00:04:10,981 --> 00:04:15,340
simply because of the way they understood the body at the time.

69
00:04:15,341 --> 00:04:16,480
Couldn't be too dry.

70
00:04:16,481 --> 00:04:18,080
It couldn't be too moist.

71
00:04:18,081 --> 00:04:21,580
So they used exercise a lot to regulate

72
00:04:21,581 --> 00:04:24,280
the temperature of the body.

73
00:04:24,281 --> 00:04:27,600
Athens also saw the advent of the gymnasium,

74
00:04:27,601 --> 00:04:29,840
Different from modern era gyms, they

75
00:04:29,841 --> 00:04:33,080
were important social centers devoted to both physical

76
00:04:33,081 --> 00:04:36,640
and intellectual development.

77
00:04:36,641 --> 00:04:40,940
While physical educators called pedatribes supervised exercise

78
00:04:40,941 --> 00:04:44,060
and competitive sport, the gymnasiums also

79
00:04:44,061 --> 00:04:48,140
hosted lectures and discussions led by philosophers,

80
00:04:48,141 --> 00:04:49,820
including Aristotle.

81
00:04:49,821 --> 00:04:54,340
The equal emphasis on physical and mental development,

82
00:04:54,341 --> 00:04:56,520
that was their greatest legacy for us

83
00:04:56,521 --> 00:04:58,180
in terms of physical fitness.

84
00:04:58,181 --> 00:05:02,060
Those gymnasiums gave rise to the university.

85
00:05:02,061 --> 00:05:07,960
And the University of Athens, its origin is in the gymnasium.

86
00:05:07,961 --> 00:05:10,240
The value placed on physical fitness

87
00:05:10,241 --> 00:05:13,380
was greatly diminished as the Greeks lost their autonomy

88
00:05:13,381 --> 00:05:17,420
to the Romans in the second century BC.

89
00:05:17,421 --> 00:05:21,300
The Romans placed physical activity in the realm of slaves.

90
00:05:21,301 --> 00:05:24,480
They declared the Olympic Games a sacrilegious ritual

91
00:05:24,481 --> 00:05:29,180
to pagan gods and banned them in 394 AD.

92
00:05:29,181 --> 00:05:31,220
The Greek training methods were practiced

93
00:05:31,221 --> 00:05:33,880
not for the development of mind and body,

94
00:05:33,881 --> 00:05:40,160
but for barbaric entertainment, such as gladiator contests.

95
00:05:40,161 --> 00:05:43,860
As Europe moved through the dark ages of the next 10 centuries,

96
00:05:43,861 --> 00:05:46,960
exercise was reserved for soldiers.

97
00:05:46,961 --> 00:05:50,240
The demands of feudal lords gave the peasants who served them

98
00:05:50,241 --> 00:05:54,140
little time for any activity beyond work.

99
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:56,800
Even as Europeans began to settle the New World,

100
00:05:57,220 --> 00:05:59,820
exercise was viewed as mere folly.

101
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:09,000
In the 1630s and 1640s in colonial America,

102
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:13,760
we had two things going on that really decreased our interest

103
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:16,620
in sports and also physical activity.

104
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,760
Number one, we didn't need extra physical activity

105
00:06:19,761 --> 00:06:21,380
because we were so busy working.

106
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:25,380
And then secondly, there was the Puritan ethic that said,

107
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:27,680
only work was valued.

108
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:32,620
Idleness was synonymous with sinning as was playing.

109
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:34,720
By the mid-1800s, Americans were

110
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:38,740
beginning their migration from the farm to the city,

111
00:06:38,840 --> 00:06:42,940
a trek that would not only start to unravel the Puritan ethic,

112
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:46,440
but see an onset of mass illness categorized

113
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:49,580
as nervous exhaustion.

114
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:53,920
Symptoms included sleeplessness, despondency, and anxiety,

115
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:57,420
accompanied by head, back, and limb pain.

116
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:01,720
Doctors placed the blame on socioeconomic progress

117
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:05,120
and what they described as the overbrain work required

118
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:06,940
for new professional pursuits.

119
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,640
One such victim was Katherine Beecher,

120
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:15,580
an educator whose health began to deteriorate

121
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:19,340
after the death of her fiance in a shipwreck.

122
00:07:19,341 --> 00:07:22,180
While her younger sister, Harriet Beecher Stowe,

123
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:24,980
was writing the century's most controversial book,

124
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:28,680
Uncle Tom's Cabin, Catherine was finding relief

125
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:31,580
from nervous exhaustion.

126
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:35,080
It came in the form of Swedish gymnastics,

127
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:37,900
a graceful form of exercise and balance

128
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,600
introduced to the US in the mid-1800s.

129
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:45,900
It was not just like bending over and touching your toes.

130
00:07:45,901 --> 00:07:49,240
they were rhythmic drills set to music,

131
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:51,800
often using light implements, not too

132
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:53,840
unlike our modern aerobic dance.

133
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:55,240
They marched around the room.

134
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:58,020
They used wands, various long sticks,

135
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:59,840
some of them which had little balls on the end,

136
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:02,660
like little miniature barbells.

137
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:04,320
They usually had a pianist who would come in

138
00:08:04,420 --> 00:08:06,720
or somebody would play a drum, and they would do these group

139
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:08,720
exercise sessions.

140
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:10,660
Beecher spent eight weeks training

141
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:13,820
with Dr. George Taylor at the Institute of the Swedish

142
00:08:13,821 --> 00:08:16,760
the Swedish movement cure in Manhattan,

143
00:08:16,761 --> 00:08:22,120
where even broomsticks could be employed for group calisthenics.

144
00:08:22,121 --> 00:08:25,580
Taylor's instruction manual illustrates the type of training

145
00:08:25,581 --> 00:08:29,060
Beecher experienced.

146
00:08:29,061 --> 00:08:33,300
Taylor put her on a resistance training physical therapy

147
00:08:33,301 --> 00:08:34,060
program.

148
00:08:34,061 --> 00:08:35,880
She did regular exercise.

149
00:08:35,881 --> 00:08:37,940
And she writes in her book with great pride

150
00:08:37,941 --> 00:08:40,500
about the fact that her waistline grew by, I think,

151
00:08:40,501 --> 00:08:42,340
five inches.

152
00:08:42,341 --> 00:08:44,340
Now, there's almost no woman I can think of today

153
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:45,940
who would report that with pride.

154
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:49,340
But for her, it was showing that the exercise worked.

155
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:52,580
Taylor advocated the use of early fitness machines,

156
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:57,280
such as the combination couch, an early form of traction.

157
00:08:57,380 --> 00:09:00,100
The adjustable bench used strong rubber bands

158
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:03,600
to pull the spine into alignment.

159
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:06,900
One of his favorite machines was the chest expander.

160
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,940
As a patient met resistance by pushing a lever,

161
00:09:09,941 --> 00:09:14,180
Taylor would slap their rib cage in order to vibrate the lungs

162
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:16,340
and free up airspace.

163
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:19,080
Perhaps Taylor's greatest contribution to fitness

164
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:22,480
was restoring Catherine Beecher to health.

165
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:25,820
She became one of its most influential and outspoken

166
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:29,500
advocates through her numerous books and exercise manuals.

167
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,600
This 1920s newsreel footage reflects, in part,

168
00:09:36,601 --> 00:09:40,300
the kind of activity Beecher was promoting 70 years earlier.

169
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:47,640
While it might appear contrary to Victorian principles,

170
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:52,220
Beecher was not lacking in Christian fervor.

171
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:55,080
To the graduates of a teacher school, she wrote,

172
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:57,660
Teach all your pupils the laws of health

173
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:01,060
and present them as the laws of God.

174
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:03,500
Let your students know they commit a sin

175
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,000
when they violate these laws.

176
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:09,600
Beecher's views reflected a movement that would influence

177
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:11,940
physical fitness through the Civil War

178
00:10:12,040 --> 00:10:16,840
and into the 20th century, one that would put God in the gym,

179
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:19,940
a movement called muscular Christianity.

180
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:28,560
In 1890, Gus Hill was America's Indian club

181
00:10:28,660 --> 00:10:30,600
swinging champion.

182
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:33,900
While his were more elaborately decorated than most,

183
00:10:33,901 --> 00:10:38,300
Indian clubs provided a popular and effective form of exercise

184
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,340
in the mid to late 19th century.

185
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:48,520
Introduced to the West by British soldiers stationed in India,

186
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:53,280
they came in a variety of sizes and weights.

187
00:10:53,380 --> 00:10:56,820
Instruction guides showed how their use would develop

188
00:10:56,920 --> 00:10:59,660
upper body strength.

189
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:03,100
Also common to the era were wooden racks called Swedish

190
00:11:03,101 --> 00:11:05,040
stall bars.

191
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:07,600
Bolted to gymnasium walls, they were

192
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:10,540
used for a regimen of lifts and pulls designed

193
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:15,040
to work the legs, arms, and abdominals.

194
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:19,040
Stall bars and Indian clubs were common in gymnasiums,

195
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:21,960
which more than two millennia after their introduction

196
00:11:22,060 --> 00:11:25,760
in Greece were finally prospering in the northeastern cities

197
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:28,360
of the United States.

198
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:31,400
One reason for their growth was an awareness of how poorly

199
00:11:31,401 --> 00:11:35,840
condition many men were during the Civil War.

200
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:40,580
Another was the need for new social gathering places.

201
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:42,520
Traditional ones, such as churches,

202
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:46,320
were diminished by urban migration.

203
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:50,220
Yet morality and religion had a place in the post-war gymnasium

204
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:54,320
due to a movement called muscular Christianity.

205
00:11:54,420 --> 00:11:57,820
This was really the beginning of what we would understand

206
00:11:57,821 --> 00:12:01,520
today as sports builds character.

207
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:03,860
The proponents of muscular Christianity

208
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:07,660
viewed fitness and health as a moral obligation.

209
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:11,260
Taking proper care of one's body was an act of gratitude

210
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:13,500
to God.

211
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:17,480
The movement took root in 1857 with the publication

212
00:12:17,580 --> 00:12:21,840
of a popular children's book, Tom Brown's School Days,

213
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:24,680
written by British author Thomas Hughes.

214
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:27,580
The book focused on boys who strengthened their bodies

215
00:12:27,581 --> 00:12:30,780
and their moral fiber through sport.

216
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:35,480
Young boys would interact with other boys, play on a team.

217
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:36,920
They would learn to share.

218
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:39,720
They would learn values.

219
00:12:39,820 --> 00:12:43,160
The movement would gain further momentum in the 1860s

220
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:46,860
after being adopted by the Young Men's Christian Association,

221
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:48,900
or YMCA.

222
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,240
After the Muscular Christianity movement

223
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:56,780
had gotten started, YMCA's in New York City and Boston

224
00:12:56,781 --> 00:13:01,580
added gymnasiums in order to attract young boys.

225
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:07,360
And this was the philosophy upon which their triangle of mind,

226
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:09,300
body, and spirit was based.

227
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:18,240
As muscular Christianity was helping

228
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:22,800
make fitness a moral exercise, Dr. Diocletian Lewis,

229
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:26,080
who often wore a heavy crown to strengthen neck muscles,

230
00:13:26,081 --> 00:13:29,620
muscles was on his way to emphasizing how much fun it

231
00:13:29,720 --> 00:13:30,780
could be.

232
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:33,280
Lewis is a fascinating character.

233
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:36,220
He's a large, charismatic guy.

234
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:39,520
He's married to a woman who has had tuberculosis.

235
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:41,420
He's not a regular physician.

236
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:43,440
He's a homeopathic physician.

237
00:13:43,540 --> 00:13:45,760
And he decided that he was going to cure his wife's

238
00:13:45,860 --> 00:13:47,840
tuberculosis through exercise.

239
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:52,340
By prescribing alternative medications

240
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:55,480
and rigorous workouts that included sawing and chopping

241
00:13:55,481 --> 00:14:01,360
Lewis saw his wife not only defeat TB, but begin to thrive.

242
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:04,860
By the early 1860s, Lewis was in constant demand,

243
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:09,500
leading nightly exercise classes throughout Boston.

244
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:13,460
He was one of the first to encourage exercise for children,

245
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:17,640
and his classes and lectures were often family affairs.

246
00:14:17,740 --> 00:14:21,080
He was apparently an extremely persuasive speaker

247
00:14:21,180 --> 00:14:23,240
and an exuberant sort of man.

248
00:14:23,241 --> 00:14:24,140
sort of man.

249
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:26,540
And everybody said that when you went to one of his classes

250
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:29,480
that you just left feeling so great that you would just do

251
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:32,280
whatever he wanted to do, and it was all fun.

252
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:35,180
Lewis called his system, which included everything

253
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:39,080
from complex ring exercises to working out with beanbags,

254
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:42,500
Indian clubs, and stretching, new gymnastics.

255
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:49,640
The nation's largest teachers organization

256
00:14:49,641 --> 00:14:53,240
called for its inclusion in all schools.

257
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:56,280
The endorsement prompted Lewis to start the first training

258
00:14:56,380 --> 00:15:01,220
institute exclusively for PE instructors at Boston's Essex

259
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:04,980
Street Gym in 1861.

260
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:07,560
He was also perhaps the first fitness guru

261
00:15:07,660 --> 00:15:11,460
to understand the power of mass media.

262
00:15:11,560 --> 00:15:14,860
He authored several books on fitness, including weak lungs

263
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:18,260
and how to make them strong, and the bestselling

264
00:15:18,261 --> 00:15:25,260
New Gymnastics for Men, Women, and Children, published in 1862.

265
00:15:25,360 --> 00:15:30,180
Lewis also printed an equally popular fitness magazine.

266
00:15:30,280 --> 00:15:32,940
But he would share a sad realization

267
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:36,520
with past and future fitness educators.

268
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:38,920
If people could take an easier, quicker route

269
00:15:39,020 --> 00:15:41,880
to fitness and health, they would.

270
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:46,880
In the mid-1800s, they sought it from inside a bottle.

271
00:15:46,881 --> 00:15:49,820
Most people would respond much more readily

272
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:53,580
to some medication that they could take without any sacrifice

273
00:15:53,680 --> 00:15:55,920
than they would to making the effort to exercise

274
00:15:56,020 --> 00:15:57,820
or to control their appetites.

275
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:00,660
A lot of people grew rich selling tonics of one sort

276
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:02,700
or another.

277
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:06,300
These tonics were referred to as patent medicine,

278
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:09,200
the precursor to over-the-counter drugs,

279
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:12,180
though history would regard their manufacturers

280
00:16:12,280 --> 00:16:14,600
as snake oil salesmen.

281
00:16:14,601 --> 00:16:17,800
Since the most common ingredient was pure alcohol,

282
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:22,540
any transformative effect was usually intoxication.

283
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:25,820
A favorite marketing ploy was to represent the potions

284
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:29,220
as secret remedies of Native Americans.

285
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:34,180
The Indians seemed to represent a pure race that had lived

286
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:37,020
in accord with nature and had an intuitive feeling

287
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:41,160
for what medicinals from nature would work.

288
00:16:41,260 --> 00:16:42,920
Despite the efforts of those who

289
00:16:42,921 --> 00:16:46,120
who would promote laziness in the guise of health,

290
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:49,920
the 19th century would end with a tremendous fitness boon.

291
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:52,160
It would occur thanks to the unlikely pairing

292
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:55,960
of a religious zealot and the man who invented cornflakes.

293
00:17:02,380 --> 00:17:05,740
At the end of the 19th century, exercise equipment

294
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:10,780
was being mass produced and sold in great numbers.

295
00:17:10,781 --> 00:17:14,120
The Milo Barbell Company became the first manufacturer

296
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:17,260
of iron weight training equipment.

297
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:19,820
Buckshot or sand poured into the dumbbell

298
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:20,820
determined the weight.

299
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:29,140
The Whitley exerciser ranging in price from $1.40 to $2

300
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:34,300
was one of the top sellers in the upper body category.

301
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:36,700
But if you asked anyone at the turn of the century

302
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:40,040
where the world's mecca of physical fitness lie,

303
00:17:40,041 --> 00:17:44,380
they would probably reply, Battle Creek, Michigan.

304
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:48,880
It was a place where exercise and cereal, enemas,

305
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:52,080
and eccentricity would all converge.

306
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:00,960
The Battle Creek fitness legacy might never

307
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:04,300
have occurred without a series of visions experienced

308
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:07,960
by Alan White, the founder of the Seventh Day Adventist

309
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:09,240
Church.

310
00:18:09,241 --> 00:18:13,380
In the 1840s, White's visions helped establish doctrine

311
00:18:13,480 --> 00:18:16,380
that encouraged vegetarianism and abstinence

312
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:20,280
from tobacco and alcohol.

313
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:22,880
20 years later, another set of visions

314
00:18:22,980 --> 00:18:26,600
compelled White to build an institution where Adventists

315
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:29,160
could be restored to health.

316
00:18:29,260 --> 00:18:32,740
In 1866, the church opened the Western Health Reform

317
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:35,340
Institute in Battle Creek.

318
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:38,340
It didn't fare very well the first 10 years or so,

319
00:18:38,341 --> 00:18:43,640
But in 1876, Ellen White appointed a new director

320
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:45,240
named John Harvey Kellogg.

321
00:18:45,340 --> 00:18:48,180
He was an extremely dynamic figure.

322
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:52,080
And he turned the fortunes of the institution around.

323
00:18:52,180 --> 00:18:55,560
A 24-year-old physician, Seventh-day Adventist,

324
00:18:55,660 --> 00:18:58,500
and devout vegetarian, Kellogg vastly

325
00:18:58,600 --> 00:19:00,660
expanded the Institute.

326
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:05,460
He renamed it the Battle Creek Sanitarium.

327
00:19:05,461 --> 00:19:07,600
It grew into a fabulous health resort.

328
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:13,160
Visitors were put on exercise programs

329
00:19:13,260 --> 00:19:17,600
and given medical examinations.

330
00:19:17,700 --> 00:19:21,100
Before long, it was known around the world simply

331
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:23,720
as the San.

332
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:26,120
You could have stopped anyone in the street by the 1890s

333
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:27,420
and asked them, what's the San?

334
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:28,620
And they would have known.

335
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:31,960
It was the best known health institution in the country.

336
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,460
At its peak, the San had as many

337
00:19:34,461 --> 00:19:41,900
as 1,400 registered patients and guests and a staff of 1,800.

338
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,600
The expansive gymnasiums, luxurious guest rooms,

339
00:19:46,700 --> 00:19:52,920
lobbies, and dining halls appealed to society's elite.

340
00:19:53,020 --> 00:19:57,120
Group calisthenics and long morning walks were daily

341
00:19:57,220 --> 00:19:57,920
rituals.

342
00:20:00,860 --> 00:20:02,820
Kellogg believed most of his clients

343
00:20:02,821 --> 00:20:06,900
suffered from a condition called auto-intoxication,

344
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
a type of internal poisoning caused

345
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:13,760
by the consumption of meat and processed foods.

346
00:20:13,860 --> 00:20:16,540
While Kellogg's treatment was considered cutting edge

347
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:20,900
at the time, it is often looked back on as eccentric.

348
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,140
He insisted his patients practice

349
00:20:23,240 --> 00:20:27,080
fletcherism, or chewing food hundreds of times

350
00:20:27,180 --> 00:20:30,040
before swallowing.

351
00:20:30,041 --> 00:20:34,680
Kellogg also believed in enemas, lots of enemas.

352
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:38,840
He gave enemas quite generously at the San.

353
00:20:38,940 --> 00:20:42,980
In fact, he had a huge machine that four patients

354
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:45,580
could be hooked up to.

355
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:50,160
You could crank them through several shifts a day.

356
00:20:50,260 --> 00:20:53,060
The regimen sometimes included yogurt enemas

357
00:20:53,160 --> 00:20:56,140
in hopes that the healthy bacteria found in yogurt

358
00:20:56,240 --> 00:20:59,800
would displace the unhealthy bacteria born in the colon

359
00:20:59,801 --> 00:21:03,140
due to a poor diet.

360
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:06,300
The sand was also the site of another popular health

361
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:10,480
trend of the late 1800s, a supposedly therapeutic system

362
00:21:10,580 --> 00:21:15,220
of bathing and water treatments called hydropathy.

363
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:17,820
More than just hygiene, it was believed

364
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:21,920
mere water applied through a variety of prescribed methods

365
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:23,860
could flush the body of impurities.

366
00:21:23,861 --> 00:21:26,760
and impurities.

367
00:21:26,860 --> 00:21:30,500
Hydropathy was believed to cure everything from syphilis

368
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:32,700
to epilepsy.

369
00:21:32,800 --> 00:21:35,640
Every part of the body would be bathed,

370
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:41,940
and the water applications could be given by pouring water over

371
00:21:42,040 --> 00:21:46,680
or putting water-soaked sheets to a specific part of the body.

372
00:21:46,780 --> 00:21:48,680
And there were also full body applications

373
00:21:48,780 --> 00:21:50,280
that were supposed to stimulate the body

374
00:21:50,380 --> 00:21:53,860
to expel the contaminating matter.

375
00:21:53,861 --> 00:21:55,660
One of Kellogg's biggest challenges

376
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:59,200
was getting patients to break their addiction to meat.

377
00:21:59,300 --> 00:22:02,600
The result would be his greatest legacy.

378
00:22:02,700 --> 00:22:06,080
He wrote entire books on the importance of breakfast

379
00:22:06,180 --> 00:22:10,780
and sought to develop substitutes for bacon and eggs.

380
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:13,420
The result was cereal.

381
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:15,780
Kellogg and his brother Will began producing

382
00:22:15,880 --> 00:22:18,580
a variety of toasted wheat and cornflakes

383
00:22:18,680 --> 00:22:22,180
that made their names famous.

384
00:22:22,181 --> 00:22:25,880
In 1891, a troubled inventor checked into the San

385
00:22:25,980 --> 00:22:27,720
and underwent treatment.

386
00:22:27,820 --> 00:22:30,920
His name was C.W. Post.

387
00:22:31,020 --> 00:22:33,760
The next year, he would open his own health clinic

388
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:35,460
in Battle Creek.

389
00:22:35,560 --> 00:22:38,400
Three years later, Post would compete with Kellogg

390
00:22:38,500 --> 00:22:41,300
on the cereal front as well.

391
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:45,540
Post Toasties and Grape Nuts would battle Kellogg's immensely

392
00:22:45,640 --> 00:22:49,800
popular shredded crumbles as the nation's favorite new health

393
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:50,980
foods.

394
00:22:50,981 --> 00:22:53,280
Future cereal makers and health resorts

395
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:56,820
would continue to open shop in Battle Creek.

396
00:22:56,920 --> 00:23:01,260
It was accepted by 1910 if you wanted to be taken seriously

397
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:03,600
as a health crusader, no matter what your particular shtick

398
00:23:03,700 --> 00:23:07,060
was, you had to have a Battle Creek connection.

399
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:10,340
John Harvey Kellogg presided as director of the sand

400
00:23:10,440 --> 00:23:14,540
for 62 years, until financial problems brought its closure

401
00:23:14,640 --> 00:23:16,580
in 1938.

402
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:20,240
He would be remembered as one of the world's most influential

403
00:23:20,241 --> 00:23:21,980
fitness gurus.

404
00:23:22,080 --> 00:23:25,480
Yet the combination of time and technology

405
00:23:25,580 --> 00:23:26,980
would bring a new wave.

406
00:23:30,780 --> 00:23:33,880
In the years just prior to World War I,

407
00:23:33,980 --> 00:23:37,060
migration to the congestion and sedentary lifestyle

408
00:23:37,160 --> 00:23:40,620
of big cities continued in greater numbers than ever

409
00:23:40,720 --> 00:23:42,000
before.

410
00:23:42,100 --> 00:23:44,560
Automobiles had replaced walking.

411
00:23:44,660 --> 00:23:47,300
And even for women, monotonous assembly lines

412
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:50,240
replaced working outdoors.

413
00:23:50,241 --> 00:23:53,980
Some city dwellers were well aware that their new way

414
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:56,240
of living would take years off their lives

415
00:23:56,340 --> 00:23:58,980
and turn their muscles to fat if they

416
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:01,980
did not exercise their bodies.

417
00:24:02,080 --> 00:24:04,280
Those who understood that probably

418
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:08,300
knew all about Bernard McFadden and the fitness magazine

419
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:13,000
he started in 1898 called Physical Culture.

420
00:24:13,100 --> 00:24:17,000
After one year of publishing articles on exercise, diet,

421
00:24:17,100 --> 00:24:19,400
and weightlifting, physical culture

422
00:24:19,401 --> 00:24:23,240
Echoculture circulation was 3,000.

423
00:24:23,340 --> 00:24:25,100
By the end of the second year, McFadden

424
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:29,340
had more than 100,000 subscribers.

425
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:33,140
Everybody who was interested in physical fitness read it.

426
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:35,340
I mean, he's a major figure.

427
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:37,160
And the fact was that he built it all

428
00:24:37,260 --> 00:24:39,060
on physical fitness and exercise.

429
00:24:39,160 --> 00:24:40,860
I mean, it's just unheard of.

430
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:43,660
He also gave a lot of space in the magazine

431
00:24:43,760 --> 00:24:46,700
to the personal stories of women who did exercise.

432
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:49,400
And there were a number of women who wrote about using

433
00:24:49,401 --> 00:24:51,700
as a way to be a better athlete or a swimmer

434
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:53,000
or to restore their health.

435
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:54,540
And those were, I think, very important messages

436
00:24:54,640 --> 00:24:57,200
because women were not getting those messages

437
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:59,540
in many other places.

438
00:24:59,640 --> 00:25:03,940
Physical culture was completely unique in the early 1900s.

439
00:25:04,040 --> 00:25:06,160
It glorified the body.

440
00:25:06,260 --> 00:25:10,060
Naked women and men, often including McFadden himself,

441
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:14,260
adorned the pages illustrating how mere mortals could gain

442
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:17,960
the bodily proportions of the Greek gods.

443
00:25:17,961 --> 00:25:20,860
When you look at the legacy of McFadden,

444
00:25:20,960 --> 00:25:22,700
it's an enormous legacy.

445
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:25,200
He popularized health foods.

446
00:25:25,300 --> 00:25:28,140
He brought into mainstream America

447
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:33,080
the idea of doing exercise as a way to increase health.

448
00:25:33,180 --> 00:25:35,640
Now, there had been plenty of school physical educators,

449
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:40,140
but it's really McFadden who takes that message to the masses.

450
00:25:40,240 --> 00:25:43,620
In spite of McFadden's widespread popularity,

451
00:25:43,720 --> 00:25:47,960
World War I proved a lasting truth.

452
00:25:47,961 --> 00:25:52,140
that far more people claim to exercise than actually do.

453
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:01,400
As President Woodrow Wilson draws numbers

454
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:04,280
to select America's war draftees,

455
00:26:04,380 --> 00:26:08,420
little does he know that so many will not make the grade.

456
00:26:08,520 --> 00:26:14,920
Millions of young men failed their draft physical exams,

457
00:26:14,921 --> 00:26:18,360
whether it was eyesight or hearing or flat feet, whatever

458
00:26:18,460 --> 00:26:24,300
it was, the message was is that our American young men

459
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:26,300
are not fit.

460
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:28,200
They are not healthy.

461
00:26:28,300 --> 00:26:32,100
They are not strong enough, physically fit enough

462
00:26:32,200 --> 00:26:33,900
to defend their own country.

463
00:26:38,940 --> 00:26:40,740
In spite of the astonishing results

464
00:26:40,840 --> 00:26:42,680
of the physical exams, Americans

465
00:26:42,681 --> 00:26:46,920
prove themselves more than capable in war.

466
00:26:47,020 --> 00:26:50,460
However, the draft experience, at least for a short time,

467
00:26:50,560 --> 00:26:54,900
prompted an increased demand for physical fitness.

468
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,120
Manufacturers such as Spalding began

469
00:26:57,220 --> 00:27:00,460
printing 200-page catalogs, offering everything

470
00:27:00,560 --> 00:27:05,740
from entire home gymnasium sets to weight machines

471
00:27:05,840 --> 00:27:12,680
to the latest in fitness fashion and athletic shoes.

472
00:27:12,681 --> 00:27:16,680
Besides being a wonderfully crafted work of art,

473
00:27:16,780 --> 00:27:19,720
the seat of health provided an effective workout

474
00:27:19,820 --> 00:27:22,320
for the entire body.

475
00:27:22,420 --> 00:27:26,760
At $50, it was a bargain considering it still works.

476
00:27:26,860 --> 00:27:31,000
Today, it's housed at the University of Texas at Austin

477
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:35,940
alongside a stationary bike of the same era.

478
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:40,140
Priced at $42, it came with a large wooden chart

479
00:27:40,141 --> 00:27:43,880
wouldn't chart for tracking mileage.

480
00:27:43,980 --> 00:27:46,780
Pumps activated by the pedals controlled the dial.

481
00:27:52,720 --> 00:27:56,820
A proliferation of other machines marketed mainly to women

482
00:27:56,920 --> 00:28:00,100
as a way to shake off those extra pounds

483
00:28:00,200 --> 00:28:02,120
were absolutely useless.

484
00:28:06,060 --> 00:28:09,140
However, women would begin to prove to themselves,

485
00:28:09,141 --> 00:28:13,980
if not the public, that they could match the strength of men.

486
00:28:13,981 --> 00:28:17,340
Gertrude Ederle made such a statement in 1926

487
00:28:17,341 --> 00:28:20,240
by being the first woman to successfully swim

488
00:28:20,241 --> 00:28:22,040
the English Channel.

489
00:28:22,041 --> 00:28:24,180
Six years later, Babe Diederiksen

490
00:28:24,181 --> 00:28:27,980
excited the world in the 1932 Los Angeles Olympics

491
00:28:27,981 --> 00:28:32,180
by breaking records in the javelin and hurdles.

492
00:28:32,181 --> 00:28:35,200
She'd cap it off by becoming one of the greatest professional

493
00:28:35,201 --> 00:28:37,700
female golfers to ever compete.

494
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:43,040
The inspiration these women provided would prove crucial.

495
00:28:43,140 --> 00:28:47,040
Another world war was brewing, and this time, the women

496
00:28:47,140 --> 00:28:48,580
would have a much greater role.

497
00:29:06,140 --> 00:29:08,040
So we saw women training not only

498
00:29:08,041 --> 00:29:11,180
not only to be good citizens, but to actually prepare

499
00:29:11,181 --> 00:29:12,680
for being a part of the military.

500
00:29:12,681 --> 00:29:14,720
And also to go and run the factories,

501
00:29:14,721 --> 00:29:17,820
because when a number of men would be going off to war,

502
00:29:17,821 --> 00:29:19,660
women were always the ones who would go in

503
00:29:19,661 --> 00:29:20,980
and run the industries.

504
00:29:20,981 --> 00:29:26,860
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, six, six.

505
00:29:30,860 --> 00:29:33,400
Women were heavily recruited for all branches

506
00:29:33,401 --> 00:29:35,840
of the military during World War II,

507
00:29:35,841 --> 00:29:38,240
and much of their training would be just as demanding

508
00:29:38,340 --> 00:29:39,940
as the men's.

509
00:29:40,040 --> 00:29:42,420
However, just as in the First World War,

510
00:29:42,520 --> 00:29:44,620
men would have a difficult time passing

511
00:29:44,720 --> 00:29:47,980
their physical entry exams.

512
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:53,660
Again, virtually millions of young boys, young men, 17,

513
00:29:53,760 --> 00:29:56,820
18, 19, failed.

514
00:29:56,920 --> 00:30:00,700
And again, this caused great alarm.

515
00:30:00,800 --> 00:30:03,160
That alarm would be addressed years later

516
00:30:03,161 --> 00:30:07,600
none other than the commander of the victorious Allied Forces.

517
00:30:07,700 --> 00:30:10,360
When he became president, Dwight Eisenhower

518
00:30:10,460 --> 00:30:13,000
received a report on the physical condition

519
00:30:13,100 --> 00:30:15,500
of America's youth.

520
00:30:15,600 --> 00:30:21,420
And what they found was that 56% of the children failed.

521
00:30:21,520 --> 00:30:23,520
And they were a bit alarmed, but they said, well,

522
00:30:23,620 --> 00:30:26,220
we better verify to see how our children compare

523
00:30:26,320 --> 00:30:27,860
with children in other parts of the world.

524
00:30:27,960 --> 00:30:29,920
They went to Austria, Italy, Switzerland,

525
00:30:30,020 --> 00:30:32,260
and administered these tests as well.

526
00:30:32,261 --> 00:30:36,700
And what they found was that only 8% of those children

527
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:38,400
failed.

528
00:30:38,500 --> 00:30:41,740
To make matters worse, newsreel reports like this one

529
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:45,380
warned of the extremes the enemy would go through.

530
00:30:45,480 --> 00:30:47,200
On the streets and in the factories,

531
00:30:47,300 --> 00:30:51,480
Eastern Europeans were spending their lunch hours shaping up.

532
00:30:51,580 --> 00:30:54,180
Even without the plethora of exercise equipment

533
00:30:54,280 --> 00:30:56,920
available in the West, communist workers

534
00:30:57,020 --> 00:31:00,580
would get by just fine with watermelon.

535
00:31:00,581 --> 00:31:02,580
In response, President Eisenhower

536
00:31:02,680 --> 00:31:07,080
formed the President's Council on Physical Fitness in 1956.

537
00:31:07,180 --> 00:31:11,260
Schools would implement new physical education programs

538
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:13,220
and monitor the progress of students

539
00:31:13,320 --> 00:31:15,620
through annual testing.

540
00:31:15,720 --> 00:31:19,560
While the 50s brought a Cold War scare to physical fitness,

541
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:22,060
it also marked a passing of the torch

542
00:31:22,160 --> 00:31:27,680
between the century's two most famous fitness gurus.

543
00:31:27,681 --> 00:31:31,980
This 1952 newsreel captures Bernard McFadden

544
00:31:32,080 --> 00:31:34,820
on his 84th birthday.

545
00:31:34,920 --> 00:31:38,020
He's celebrating by skydiving over Paris

546
00:31:38,120 --> 00:31:41,860
to prove the long-term benefits of the physical culture

547
00:31:41,960 --> 00:31:42,800
lifestyle.

548
00:31:49,260 --> 00:31:52,740
McFadden's leap is perhaps a final farewell

549
00:31:52,840 --> 00:31:56,640
to the millions of faithful who loved his physical culture

550
00:31:56,641 --> 00:31:59,340
magazine.

551
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:02,340
There's no telling how popular McFadden could have been

552
00:32:02,440 --> 00:32:05,440
if he had a medium like television with which

553
00:32:05,540 --> 00:32:07,480
to convey his message.

554
00:32:07,580 --> 00:32:12,380
But America's newest fitness guru was about to find out.

555
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:16,200
It's time for the Jack LaLanne Show from Hollywood.

556
00:32:16,300 --> 00:32:18,900
Thanks very, very much for letting me come into your home.

557
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:21,000
You know, my name is Jack LaLanne.

558
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:23,800
And I'm here for one reason and one reason only,

559
00:32:23,900 --> 00:32:26,400
to show you how to feel better and look better

560
00:32:26,401 --> 00:32:28,400
so you can live longer, please.

561
00:32:28,500 --> 00:32:31,740
The Jack LaLanne Show premiered in 1951,

562
00:32:31,840 --> 00:32:34,900
making television the most technologically advanced

563
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:36,940
fitness tool ever created.

564
00:32:37,040 --> 00:32:40,340
One, two, three, four, one.

565
00:32:40,440 --> 00:32:43,560
Jack LaLanne came on the scene, and what's interesting is he

566
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:48,260
was delivering a message that many doctors and many people

567
00:32:48,360 --> 00:32:51,660
said were short-lived, and he would be off the air

568
00:32:51,760 --> 00:32:52,960
within six weeks.

569
00:32:52,961 --> 00:32:56,400
Well, Jack ran for 34 years.

570
00:32:56,500 --> 00:32:58,200
And I was strict vegetarian.

571
00:32:58,300 --> 00:33:00,360
So everybody else crackpot that Jack LaLanne.

572
00:33:00,460 --> 00:33:01,800
I was into juicing.

573
00:33:01,900 --> 00:33:03,500
And you know, I was a nut.

574
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:04,400
I was a filbert.

575
00:33:04,500 --> 00:33:05,800
I was a crackpot.

576
00:33:05,900 --> 00:33:06,900
That just shows you, right?

577
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:10,180
Because I'm going to be with you every day this hour.

578
00:33:10,280 --> 00:33:12,780
And I sure appreciate your support, which I need very,

579
00:33:12,880 --> 00:33:13,840
very badly.

580
00:33:13,940 --> 00:33:15,080
If you appreciate what I'm doing, coming into your home

581
00:33:15,180 --> 00:33:17,560
every day like this, just tell your friend about the Jack

582
00:33:17,660 --> 00:33:20,880
LaLanne was a natural, totally unscripted,

583
00:33:20,881 --> 00:33:24,320
his enthusiasm for demonstrating how to get in shape at home

584
00:33:24,420 --> 00:33:26,820
was the result of many years of training,

585
00:33:26,920 --> 00:33:29,620
owning gyms, and proper nutrition.

586
00:33:29,720 --> 00:33:31,120
You see, my life was saved.

587
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:34,520
I was a skinny, weak, miserable, sugar-holy kid.

588
00:33:34,620 --> 00:33:36,900
At the age of 15, I attended a health lecture,

589
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:40,060
and I quit eating meat, and I ate natural foods,

590
00:33:40,160 --> 00:33:42,260
and started exercising my whole life chain.

591
00:33:42,360 --> 00:33:43,840
If something saved your life, would you

592
00:33:43,940 --> 00:33:45,240
be enthusiastic about it?

593
00:33:45,340 --> 00:33:48,840
At age 84, Lallane is a living testament

594
00:33:48,841 --> 00:33:52,080
to the fitness virtues he espoused nearly 50 years

595
00:33:52,180 --> 00:33:55,140
earlier when his television show first aired.

596
00:33:55,240 --> 00:33:58,980
I think in the 50s, many women were housewives at the time.

597
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:01,880
And certainly, they were not going to gyms.

598
00:34:01,980 --> 00:34:05,720
They weren't even going to exercise studios much at the time.

599
00:34:05,820 --> 00:34:09,000
And what they did with Jack was, I mean,

600
00:34:09,100 --> 00:34:10,200
a new form of movement.

601
00:34:10,300 --> 00:34:13,500
I remember my mother exercising in front of the TV.

602
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:16,160
At age 40, Lelaine began a tradition

603
00:34:16,161 --> 00:34:20,800
of spectacular birthday feats that would last into his 70s.

604
00:34:20,900 --> 00:34:24,560
He swam from the inescapable Alcatraz Island prison

605
00:34:24,660 --> 00:34:27,100
to San Francisco in handcuffs.

606
00:34:27,200 --> 00:34:30,300
This is swimming the tough way across more than two miles

607
00:34:30,400 --> 00:34:33,620
of choppy water that's a chill 48 degrees.

608
00:34:33,720 --> 00:34:35,820
Lallane's birthday conquests were

609
00:34:35,920 --> 00:34:38,620
to call publicity to the amazing possibilities

610
00:34:38,720 --> 00:34:40,720
that come with fitness.

611
00:34:40,820 --> 00:34:43,260
But it was his talent for bringing exercise

612
00:34:43,261 --> 00:34:47,160
to the privacy of thousands of living rooms each week

613
00:34:47,260 --> 00:34:49,760
that made Jack LaLanne a fitness legend.

614
00:34:53,900 --> 00:34:56,840
LaLanne's birthday conquests were to call publicity

615
00:34:56,940 --> 00:35:00,820
to the amazing possibilities that come with fitness.

616
00:35:00,920 --> 00:35:03,340
But it was his talent for bringing exercise

617
00:35:03,440 --> 00:35:07,220
to the privacy of thousands of living rooms each week

618
00:35:07,320 --> 00:35:09,820
that made Jack LaLanne a fitness legend.

619
00:35:09,821 --> 00:35:11,820
and it was not imagined.

620
00:35:14,820 --> 00:35:18,820
Prior to the late 1960s, track athletes and few others

621
00:35:18,920 --> 00:35:21,060
dedicated much time to running, jogging,

622
00:35:21,160 --> 00:35:24,060
or even vigorous walking.

623
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:28,580
Those who did probably noticed that they felt better.

624
00:35:28,680 --> 00:35:30,580
They may not have realized that it

625
00:35:30,680 --> 00:35:32,980
was because they were increasing their heart rates

626
00:35:33,080 --> 00:35:35,620
and oxygen intake.

627
00:35:35,720 --> 00:35:38,380
And they certainly didn't call this type of exercise

628
00:35:38,381 --> 00:35:43,380
because prior to 1966, the word didn't exist.

629
00:35:50,920 --> 00:35:53,920
Aerobics is endurance exercise that improves

630
00:35:54,020 --> 00:35:57,440
the cardiovascular system, such as jogging, cycling,

631
00:35:57,540 --> 00:36:00,340
or swimming, or more specifically.

632
00:36:00,440 --> 00:36:02,240
Aerobics is a method of physical exercise

633
00:36:02,340 --> 00:36:05,140
for producing beneficial changes in the respiratory

634
00:36:05,240 --> 00:36:08,380
systems by activities which require only a modest increase.

635
00:36:08,381 --> 00:36:09,880
and so can be maintained.

636
00:36:09,980 --> 00:36:11,280
Where'd that come from?

637
00:36:11,380 --> 00:36:12,880
Oxford English Dictionary.

638
00:36:12,980 --> 00:36:15,780
I gave it to him back in 1985 to put it in 1986.

639
00:36:15,880 --> 00:36:16,880
That's what aerobics is.

640
00:36:20,260 --> 00:36:23,420
Dr. Kenneth Cooper is the father of aerobics.

641
00:36:23,520 --> 00:36:28,500
He coined the word in 1966 by adding an S and making a noun

642
00:36:28,600 --> 00:36:31,440
out of the adjective aerobic, meaning occurring

643
00:36:31,540 --> 00:36:35,140
in the presence of oxygen.

644
00:36:35,240 --> 00:36:38,380
Six years earlier, Cooper, an Air Force

645
00:36:38,381 --> 00:36:41,380
The Air Force flight surgeon made aerobics the centerpiece

646
00:36:41,480 --> 00:36:44,380
of a fitness program for America's astronauts.

647
00:36:47,080 --> 00:36:50,420
My responsibility was to develop a conditioning and training

648
00:36:50,520 --> 00:36:53,960
program for the astronauts prior to going into space.

649
00:36:54,060 --> 00:36:56,840
Because we realized that the rapidity with which these men

650
00:36:56,940 --> 00:36:59,300
would lose their fitness as they're in the weightlessness

651
00:36:59,400 --> 00:37:01,840
of space was directly related to the level of fitness

652
00:37:01,940 --> 00:37:03,840
before going into space.

653
00:37:03,940 --> 00:37:05,740
And it was a survival situation.

654
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:07,880
Because if those men were so deteriorated

655
00:37:07,881 --> 00:37:09,620
in space for one to two weeks.

656
00:37:09,720 --> 00:37:12,580
In a survival situation, they stand up, they faint,

657
00:37:12,680 --> 00:37:13,680
they could die.

658
00:37:13,780 --> 00:37:16,380
By the mid-60s, Cooper's aerobics program

659
00:37:16,480 --> 00:37:20,220
was adopted by the entire Air Force.

660
00:37:20,320 --> 00:37:23,560
Still, many of his colleagues were openly skeptical

661
00:37:23,660 --> 00:37:26,860
about his ideas, including the use of stress tests

662
00:37:26,960 --> 00:37:29,800
to monitor cardiovascular activity,

663
00:37:29,900 --> 00:37:34,140
and whether jogging would cause more harm than good.

664
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:36,940
I'm afraid I was quite controversial during that time.

665
00:37:36,941 --> 00:37:39,240
not just among my Air Force medical colleagues.

666
00:37:39,340 --> 00:37:42,040
There was so much skepticism among my colleagues in general.

667
00:37:42,140 --> 00:37:43,580
And physicians in general knew nothing

668
00:37:43,680 --> 00:37:44,940
about preventive medicine.

669
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:46,380
They were afraid of stress testing.

670
00:37:46,480 --> 00:37:49,180
Everything I was doing was unheard of in the military

671
00:37:49,280 --> 00:37:50,580
and the medical profession.

672
00:37:50,680 --> 00:37:53,860
Cooper persisted, continuing to improve and expand

673
00:37:53,960 --> 00:37:56,600
his aerobics programs from Lackland Air Force Base

674
00:37:56,700 --> 00:37:58,200
in San Antonio, Texas.

675
00:38:02,200 --> 00:38:04,300
He demanded as much from the officers

676
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:06,940
as he did the enlisted men.

677
00:38:06,941 --> 00:38:10,080
But while the military was getting in shape with Cooper,

678
00:38:10,180 --> 00:38:13,380
much of the public was sedentary.

679
00:38:13,480 --> 00:38:17,820
A 1968 Gallup poll estimated less than 24%

680
00:38:17,920 --> 00:38:21,060
of American adults exercised.

681
00:38:21,160 --> 00:38:23,460
That same year, Cooper described his methods

682
00:38:23,560 --> 00:38:28,900
in a thin paperback book simply titled Aerobics.

683
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:32,640
Originally sold through coupons attached to tubs of margarine,

684
00:38:32,740 --> 00:38:34,340
the book which, among other things,

685
00:38:34,341 --> 00:38:37,940
other things, exposed the aerobic benefits of jogging,

686
00:38:38,040 --> 00:38:41,140
became an overnight bestseller.

687
00:38:41,240 --> 00:38:43,540
If people start jogging all over the world,

688
00:38:43,640 --> 00:38:45,280
and there was so much concern expressed

689
00:38:45,380 --> 00:38:46,720
by my medical colleagues that people

690
00:38:46,820 --> 00:38:48,580
are going to die while out there jogging, particularly

691
00:38:48,680 --> 00:38:51,720
past 40 years of age, I would see titles

692
00:38:51,820 --> 00:38:52,960
in medical newspaper articles that

693
00:38:53,060 --> 00:38:55,360
said the streets are going to be full of dead joggers

694
00:38:55,460 --> 00:38:57,700
as more Americans follow Cooper.

695
00:38:57,800 --> 00:39:01,660
From 1968 to 1984, it is estimated

696
00:39:01,661 --> 00:39:06,300
that jogging increased from 100,000 to 34 million

697
00:39:06,400 --> 00:39:10,040
regular participants.

698
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:12,240
Coincidentally, coronary death, which

699
00:39:12,340 --> 00:39:15,300
had been increasing steadily since the 1940s,

700
00:39:15,400 --> 00:39:21,620
actually peaked in 1968 and has steadily declined ever since.

701
00:39:21,720 --> 00:39:23,120
Then we check your leg.

702
00:39:23,220 --> 00:39:24,720
Now push up against resistance.

703
00:39:24,820 --> 00:39:28,860
I'm checking for any signs of any cracking in your knee.

704
00:39:28,960 --> 00:39:31,420
In 1970, Cooper left the Air Force

705
00:39:31,421 --> 00:39:35,220
to begin his own health clinic in Dallas, Texas.

706
00:39:35,320 --> 00:39:37,660
Now the Cooper Aerobic Center is staffed

707
00:39:37,760 --> 00:39:39,960
by a team of physicians.

708
00:39:40,060 --> 00:39:43,860
The sprawling complex includes overnight accommodations

709
00:39:43,960 --> 00:39:46,560
and a gigantic fitness center.

710
00:39:46,660 --> 00:39:50,280
It harkens back to the days of the Battle Creek Sanitarium,

711
00:39:50,380 --> 00:39:53,680
except state-of-the-art exercise and medical equipment

712
00:39:53,780 --> 00:39:58,180
have replaced the quadruple enema machines.

713
00:39:58,280 --> 00:40:00,620
Cooper's influence was behind the advent

714
00:40:00,621 --> 00:40:02,920
of a fitness movement that would take hold

715
00:40:02,921 --> 00:40:05,260
in the early 1980s.

716
00:40:05,261 --> 00:40:08,620
Aerobics would soon become associated with blaring music,

717
00:40:08,621 --> 00:40:13,960
leg warmers, colorful leotards, and actress Jane Fonda.

718
00:40:13,961 --> 00:40:19,280
In 1982, Jane Fonda came out with her aerobics video.

719
00:40:19,281 --> 00:40:25,280
Well, by 1984, there were 24 million women doing aerobics,

720
00:40:25,281 --> 00:40:27,120
a huge growth in aerobics.

721
00:40:27,121 --> 00:40:28,980
Well, and we weren't doing low impact.

722
00:40:28,981 --> 00:40:30,820
We were going for the burn with Jane.

723
00:40:30,920 --> 00:40:34,380
Biceps, one, two, three.

724
00:40:34,480 --> 00:40:38,880
Much of Fonda's success was due to an advance in electronics.

725
00:40:38,980 --> 00:40:41,860
What television did for Jack LaLanne,

726
00:40:41,960 --> 00:40:46,100
the VCR did for Fonda.

727
00:40:46,200 --> 00:40:48,500
The fact that the VCR was available

728
00:40:48,600 --> 00:40:51,600
and the video was produced with Jane Fonda,

729
00:40:51,700 --> 00:40:55,740
women were now able to exercise at home in a very big way.

730
00:40:55,741 --> 00:41:00,240
and having an opportunity that they never had before in history.

731
00:41:00,340 --> 00:41:04,440
They were able to pick and choose the type of exercise program

732
00:41:04,540 --> 00:41:05,540
that they wanted to do.

733
00:41:05,640 --> 00:41:10,940
Inhale up and down.

734
00:41:11,040 --> 00:41:14,740
Fonda's videos also attracted record numbers of men and women

735
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:19,140
to health clubs to participate in group aerobics.

736
00:41:19,240 --> 00:41:22,840
Those who chose to could get a cardiovascular workout

737
00:41:22,940 --> 00:41:24,340
on their own.

738
00:41:24,341 --> 00:41:27,740
thanks to a new generation of electronic bikes,

739
00:41:27,840 --> 00:41:30,340
treadmills, and stair climbers.

740
00:41:33,340 --> 00:41:36,580
Advancements in digital technology in the mid-'80s

741
00:41:36,680 --> 00:41:40,180
allowed users to keep track of time, distance,

742
00:41:40,280 --> 00:41:44,000
and an estimate of how many calories they were burning.

743
00:41:44,100 --> 00:41:48,200
Lifecycle, one of the leading manufacturers of such machines,

744
00:41:48,300 --> 00:41:53,800
made an important advance in 1997 by adding a key feature,

745
00:41:53,801 --> 00:41:55,960
A heart rate monitor lets users know

746
00:41:56,060 --> 00:41:58,700
if they are within their target heart range

747
00:41:58,800 --> 00:42:04,480
or the level at which exercise is vigorous enough to burn fat.

748
00:42:04,580 --> 00:42:06,120
The American Council of Exercise

749
00:42:06,220 --> 00:42:08,780
states the fact that it's difficult to burn fat

750
00:42:08,880 --> 00:42:10,920
unless you're at your target heart range

751
00:42:11,020 --> 00:42:15,060
doing any cardiovascular exercise.

752
00:42:15,160 --> 00:42:17,760
Electronic sensors built into the handles

753
00:42:17,860 --> 00:42:20,200
read the user's pulse to determine

754
00:42:20,300 --> 00:42:23,000
whether the target range is being reached.

755
00:42:23,001 --> 00:42:25,800
A display screen lets them know if they are within range

756
00:42:25,900 --> 00:42:27,800
or whether they need to speed up.

757
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,700
Most people don't get up to their target heart range.

758
00:42:30,800 --> 00:42:32,440
So they're literally and figuratively

759
00:42:32,540 --> 00:42:33,740
spinning their wheels.

760
00:42:33,840 --> 00:42:35,640
What's great about this model is it

761
00:42:35,740 --> 00:42:40,360
lets me know that I'm actually burning fat.

762
00:42:40,460 --> 00:42:42,680
Digital technology is but one more

763
00:42:42,780 --> 00:42:46,060
advance in the long history of physical fitness.

764
00:42:46,160 --> 00:42:49,060
It offers another option in the pursuit of health,

765
00:42:49,160 --> 00:42:52,300
one motivated by 4,000 years of looking down

766
00:42:52,301 --> 00:42:56,480
at our waistlines and not liking what we see.

767
00:42:56,580 --> 00:42:59,180
A little flexing.

768
00:42:59,280 --> 00:43:04,480
It is also a uniquely human activity,

769
00:43:04,580 --> 00:43:07,720
creating movement not to haunt the next meal,

770
00:43:07,820 --> 00:43:11,020
but to alleviate the potentially negative effects

771
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:13,620
of the prior meal.

772
00:43:13,720 --> 00:43:17,620
It is a lifelong effort to increase longevity.

773
00:43:17,720 --> 00:43:19,460
You can't store fitness.

774
00:43:19,461 --> 00:43:23,060
Remember, fitness is a journey, not a destination.

775
00:43:23,160 --> 00:43:24,960
You've got to keep it up the rest of your life.

776
00:43:25,060 --> 00:43:26,960
It's not what you did six months ago that counts.

777
00:43:27,060 --> 00:43:29,400
It's what you did yesterday that counts.

778
00:43:29,500 --> 00:43:32,100
And yet for all we've learned about fitness,

779
00:43:32,200 --> 00:43:34,540
obesity and a sedentary lifestyle

780
00:43:34,640 --> 00:43:37,880
still threaten the health of millions.

781
00:43:37,980 --> 00:43:41,320
The Surgeon General's report came out in 1996

782
00:43:41,420 --> 00:43:44,680
calling for all Americans to be active.

783
00:43:44,780 --> 00:43:49,460
And they said it was imperative to be active for our health.

784
00:43:49,461 --> 00:43:54,780
Our salvation depends on returning to the time of the Greeks

785
00:43:54,781 --> 00:43:59,640
and embracing their philosophy of the equal development

786
00:43:59,641 --> 00:44:04,260
of the mind and body and the fundamental belief

787
00:44:04,261 --> 00:44:07,420
that that is essential for the complete development

788
00:44:07,421 --> 00:44:10,260
of every man, woman, and child.

789
